/-!
# Triples `(d, L, ψ)` and their strict equivalence

Let `S = ⨁_{k ≥ 0} 𝒮 k` be a graded ring. Section 27.12 of the Stacks project classifies the
morphisms of a scheme `Y` into `Proj S` by *triples*: an integer `d ≥ 1`, an invertible
`𝒪_Y`-module `L`, and a graded ring map

`ψ : S^{(d)} ⟶ Γ_*(Y, L) = ⨁_{n ≥ 0} Γ(Y, L^{⊗ n})`

such that `L` is generated by the global sections `ψ(f)` for `f ∈ S_d`. Two triples with the
same `d` are *strictly equivalent* when there is an isomorphism `β : L ⟶ L'` carrying `ψ` to
`ψ'`.

This file defines the triple and strict equivalence. The source's graded ring `Γ_*(Y, L)` is the
one the library already carries, `Stacks.SheafOfModules.sectionRing` with its graded commutative
ring structure `Stacks.SheafOfModules.sectionRingGradedRing` of Tag 01CV, and `S^{(d)}` is the
Veronese subring `Stacks.Veronese.veroneseSubring` with its reindexed grading, so `ψ` is a
genuine `GradedRingHom` and not a family of additive maps.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.gammaStarGrading` — the homogeneous components of
  `Γ_*(Y, L)`, as additive subgroups of the graded section ring of Tag 01CV.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.psiComponent` — the degree `n` component
  `(S^{(d)})_n ⟶ Γ(Y, L^{⊗ n})` of a graded ring map into `Γ_*(Y, L)`.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.degreeOneSection` — the global section of `L^{⊗ 1}`
  attached by such a map to a homogeneous element of degree exactly `d`; these are the sections
  `ψ(f)`, `f ∈ S_d`, of the source.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.Triple` — **the triple `(d, L, ψ)` of Tag 01N4**, with `d`
  and `L` as parameters and the graded ring map and the generation condition as fields.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.BundledTriple` — the same with `d`, its positivity and `L`
  bundled in, which is the literal reading of the source's "a triple consists of `d ≥ 1`, `L`
  and `ψ`".
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.tensorPowIsoOfIso` — the canonical isomorphism
  `L^{⊗ n} ≅ (L')^{⊗ n}` induced by an isomorphism `L ≅ L'`.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.StrictEquiv` — **strict equivalence of two triples with
  the same `d`**, Tag 01N4.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTriple.gammaStarGCommRing` — the graded commutative ring
  structure on `Γ_*(Y, L)` for an invertible `𝒪_Y`-module, re-exported at the spelling instance
  search can use on a scheme.

## Implementation notes

**Why `L` is a parameter and not a field.** The type of `ψ` mentions the graded ring `Γ_*(Y, L)`,
whose ring structure needs `L` invertible, so a structure carrying `L` as a field would have to
produce that instance from an earlier field inside the type of a later one. Keeping `L` and its
invertibility as parameters makes every statement about a triple elaborate with ordinary instance
resolution; `BundledTriple` packages them back up for the statements that quantify over all
triples, and is where the source's `d ≥ 1` lives.

**Three instances are supplied by hand**, each a restatement of an existing result at a spelling
instance search does not reach on a scheme: `Stacks.RelativeVeryAmple.stacksIsInvertible` turns
the ampleness theory's invertibility class into the one Tag 01CU's tensor powers ask for,
`Stacks.RelativeVeryAmple.isEquivalence_tensorRightFunctor_of_isInvertibleModule` supplies the
hypothesis of `Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorPower.tensorPower` itself, and
`gammaStarGCommRing` re-exports `Stacks.SheafOfModules.sectionRingGradedRing`, which does not
fire through `Stacks.SheafOfModules.sectionRing` on a scheme. No new mathematics is introduced by
any of them.

**Global sections and the terminal open.** The summands of `Stacks.SheafOfModules.sectionRing`
are the sections over the terminal object of the site of opens, which is isomorphic but not
definitionally equal to `⊤`. `toTopSection` is the canonical comparison, the restriction along
the unique morphism `⊤ ⟶ ⊤_`; it is needed because the nonvanishing locus of the ampleness theory
is stated for a section over `⊤`.

**Strict equivalence is stated degreewise**, as the equality of the two composites in each
degree. That is the source's condition `β ∘ ψ = ψ'` written out: a graded map is its family of
components. It is phrased this way deliberately, so that stating it needs only the canonical
isomorphisms `L^{⊗ n} ≅ (L')^{⊗ n}` and not their compatibility with the multiplication of the
section ring, which the library does not yet carry.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01N4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01N4)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01CV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01CV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01CU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01CU)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01N5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01N5)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

